WEBVTT

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.000
Before buying hardware or installing tools, define what the lab is for.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.920
A lab for learning local model basics has different requirements than a lab for coding

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:08.260
assistance,

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:12.235
document analysis, image generation, vision testing, automation,

00:00:12.235 --> 00:00:15.000
retrieval-augmented generation, or application prototyping.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.135
Clear goals prevent expensive builds that look impressive but do not actually support the

00:00:19.135 --> 00:00:20.250
work you want to do.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.000
Requirements should include more than model speed.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.860
Think about data sensitivity, privacy expectations, budget, power use, cooling, noise,

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.000
portability, uptime, and performance.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.455
A quiet mini PC may be better for everyday learning than a loud workstation that you avoid

00:00:33.455 --> 00:00:34.000
turning on.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.960
A laptop may be useful for travel even if it cannot run the largest models.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.660
A dedicated server may make sense only when the workload and operating expectations

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:41.978
justify it.

00:00:41.978 --> 00:00:45.000
Scope also helps decide how formal the lab should be.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.960
If the lab handles only public test prompts, you may accept looser isolation.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.000
If it handles personal documents, client files, internal notes,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.900
or security-sensitive material, you need stronger data handling, backups, access control,

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:57.960
and logging habits.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.960
Treat the lab as a data-handling environment, not just a toy machine with a GPU.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.000
A practical requirements statement can be simple.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.910
Name the primary use cases, the kind of data allowed in the lab, the expected users,

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:10.000
the acceptable budget, the desired uptime,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.000
and the services that should be reachable from other devices.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.000
This gives you a decision filter.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.000
When a new tool or hardware option appears,

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.000
you can ask whether it serves the lab's purpose or simply adds complexity.
